    "body": "# PR description\n\n- [x] I, the PR author, have personally reviewed every line of this PR.\n\n## What does this PR do?\n\nRestore the tensor-value assertion in `TestSerialization.test_empty_load`.\n\nThe test gives rank 3 the main replica containing `[3, 3]`, while other tensor replicas contain different values. After loading, it checks the returned keys but discards the result of the tensor-value comparison. Consequently, an incorrect payload such as `[4, 4]` can pass the test.\n\nAdd the missing `assert` to enforce the existing expectation that the loaded tensor contains the values saved by rank 3. The change is one line in `tests/unit_tests/dist_checkpointing/test_serialization.py`.\n\n## Issue tracking\n\nNo linked issue.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Ran `tools/autoformat.sh` in check mode: Black, isort, Pylint, Ruff, and mypy passed.\n- `git diff --check` passed.\n- Executed the test's extracted post-load validation block with real PyTorch CPU tensors: all 41 experiment checks passed. Correct payloads pass both versions; 20 corrupted-payload cases across the rank 3–7 branches incorrectly pass before the fix and raise `AssertionError` afterward. Existing common-value and key checks retain their behavior.\n\nThe isolated experiment validates assertion sensitivity. The complete distributed checkpoint save/load test remains to be run in CI: the local environment has Python 3.10 and two GPUs, while the project requires Python ≥3.12 and this test uses TP2/PP4.\n\n## Contribution process\n\n### Pre-checks\n\n- [x] Corrected the existing unit-test assertion.\n- [x] Ran `tools/autoformat.sh` in check mode.\n- [ ] Ran the complete distributed test — pending CI.\n\nFunctional tests, kernel determinism coverage, typing changes, and documentation changes are not applicable to this assertion-only fix.\n",
